=[ Sisho ]

Orchestrator CLI for the Tomet ecosystem: one entry point over `tomet`, `twrit` and `tandr`.

==[ Overview ]

`sisho` (司書, "librarian") keeps the whole project in order. It does not replace the tools it calls: `tomet` stays the language core, `twrit` enforces a repository's written rules, and `tandr` searches and rewrites structure. `sisho` runs them together.

It has no manifest of its own. Each tool already carries its own, and a need for more is a sign of a design mistake.

The design memo is @link(tmtroot/dev/design.tmt).

==[ Usage ]

```bash
sisho check [path]   # verify without writing; steps run at the same time
sisho fix [path]     # regenerate, export, format; steps run one after another
sisho new [path]     # make a document from a blueprint the vault declares
sisho check-links [path]   # find links that point at nothing (cached, outside the vault)
sisho refactor [path]      # migrate old spellings; a dry run unless -i
sisho export [path]        # write the outputs documents declare; --check compares
sisho convert <path|-> --to F | --from F   # one document, standard input or a directory, to or from Tomet
sisho serve <file>         # a document as HTML over HTTP on 127.0.0.1, rendered again per request
sisho tui [path]           # the interactive workbench: migrate Markdown, edit metadata in batches, refactor structure
sisho <tool> ...     # run a tool with the rest of the command line, unchanged
```

Both take `--json`, and `check` and `fix` take `--verbose` to show the output of steps that passed too. One failing step does not stop the others. A tool that is not installed counts as a failure.

`check` runs `tomet check`, `tomet format --check`, `twrit check`, and then `sisho`'s own `export --check` and `refactor --check`. `fix` runs `twrit check --fix`, `sisho export` and `tomet format -i`, in that order. `check-links` is not part of either: it is a command of its own. Neither is `refactor -i`, which drops comments; `check` points at it when `refactor --check` fails.

Exit codes of `check` and `fix`: 0 when every step passed, 1 when any did not, 2 when `sisho` itself could not do its job (for example a usage error).

Commands that work on the vault itself, like `new`, `check-links`, `refactor`, `export`, `convert`, `serve` and `tui`, exit 0 on success and 1 when they could not do what was asked, as `tomet` does; a usage error is 2.

When the reader of the output goes away early (`sisho check-links | head`), `sisho` ends quietly with 141, which is 128 plus `SIGPIPE`, as a shell reports it.

`sisho <tool> ...` is a pass-through: the tool gets the terminal and everything after its name, flags included, and its exit code becomes `sisho`'s. A tool that is not installed exits 127, one that cannot be started 126, one ended by a signal 128 plus the signal, as `env` does.

==[ Workspace Layout ]

-| @dir(app) (`sisho-cli`) -- Main CLI binary (`sisho`). Layer 2.
-| @dir(crates/sisho-tools) (`sisho-tools`) -- What `sisho` knows about `tomet`, `twrit` and `tandr`. Layer 1.
-| @dir(crates/sisho-vault) (`sisho-vault`) -- Vault and workspace commands moved out of `tomet`, and conversions. Layer 1.
-| @dir(crates/sisho-serve) (`sisho-serve`) -- `serve`, kept apart so the HTTP stack stays out of other builds. Layer 1.
-| @dir(crates/sisho-tui) (`sisho-tui`) -- `tui`, kept apart for the same reason. Layer 1.
-| @dir(crates/sisho-run) (`sisho-run`) -- Runs external commands and collects outcomes. The only crate that starts a process. Layer 0.

==[ Building and Testing ]

=== Build workspace
```bash
cargo build
```

=== Run tests
```bash
cargo test --workspace
```
